          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/permutations"} permutations
            , namely those which (when thought of as permuting the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers"} natural numbers
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mo ,
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{1, 2, \cdots, n \}
            = succeed "-preserving" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+order"} linear order
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            steps and then again along the remaining
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi q
                  %mo =
                  %mi n
                  %mo −
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q = n - p
            steps.
          %p
            The term ‘shuffle’ conjures up the idea of shuffling a pack of cards. In fact the mathematical idea is nearer to shuffling two packs of cards one through the other. Suppose we have a pack of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            cards and a pack of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q
            cards and we build a pack of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo +
                  %mi q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p+q
            cards, whilst retaining the order on the two ‘sub-packs’. The result is a
            = succeed "-shuffle." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi p
                    %mo ,
                    %mi q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (p,q)
          %p Or rather, the permutation in question may be seen as describing the un-doing of such a shuffling, whence some authors say “un-shuffle” for the same concept.
          %p#Illustration
            In this vein, the following graphics shows an illustration of an example of a
            = succeed "-(un-)shuffle" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 4
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 3
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (4,3)
