          %p
            From
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo &lt;
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (1 \lt 2)
            or
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mn 3
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (0\lt3)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo &lt;
                  %mn 3
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (1 \lt 3)
            and a 2-step path (left to you), and, finally it is just one step from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo &lt;
                  %mn 3
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (1 \lt 3)
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 2
                  %mo &lt;
                  %mn 3
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (2 \lt 3)
            and the other extremal path.
          %p
            %br/
          %p In summary, each path corresponds to a simplex of maximal dimension in the product. The poset encodes the simplest relationships between those paths with the links in the Hasse diagram corresponding to simple changes in the path, and adjacency of the two simplices in the product, but note that the poset is usually not linear.
          %h3#the_antilex_order The Anti-Lex order
          %p
            There is a total order on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Shuff
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi p
                  %mo ,
                  %mi q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Shuff(p,q)
            related to the above partial order and which is useful when checking cancellation of terms in non-commutative contexts, as occurs in the Whitehead product formula given by Curtis and attributed to Kan. This is the
            %strong anti-lexicographic order.
          %p
            We represent a
            = succeed "-shuffle" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi p
                    %mo ,
                    %mi q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (p,q)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi μ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ν
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mu,\nu)
            by an increasing
            = succeed "-sequence" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi μ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi μ
                      %mn 1
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mi …
                    %mo &lt;
                    %msub
                      %mi μ
                      %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu_1\lt \ldots \lt \mu_p
            = succeed "-sequence" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ν
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nu
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ν
                      %mn 1
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mi …
                    %mo &lt;
                    %msub
                      %mi ν
                      %mi q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nu_1\lt \ldots \lt \nu_q
          %p
            We order the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi μ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ν
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mu,\nu)
            using just the
            = succeed "-sequence" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi μ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            = succeed "-sequence" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ν
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nu
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi μ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi μ
                    %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≺
                  %mi μ
                  %mstyle{:scriptlevel => "0"}
                    %mo ′
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi μ
                  %msub
                    %mstyle{:scriptlevel => "0"}
                      %mo ′
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %mi μ
                  %msub
                    %mstyle{:scriptlevel => "0"}
                      %mo ′
                    %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu = (\mu_1,\ldots, \mu_p)\prec \mu\prime = (\mu\prime_1,\ldots, \mu\prime_p),
          %p
            (i) if
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi μ
                      %mi p
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mi μ
                    %msub
                      %mstyle{:scriptlevel => "0"}
                        %mo ′
                      %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu_p\lt \mu\prime_p
          %p or
          %p
            (ii) if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi μ
                    %mi p
                  %mo =
                  %mi μ
                  %msub
                    %mstyle{:scriptlevel => "0"}
                      %mo ′
                    %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu_p = \mu\prime_p
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi μ
                    %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu_p
            is odd,
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi μ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi μ
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≺
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi μ
                  %msub
                    %mstyle{:scriptlevel => "0"}
                      %mo ′
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %mi μ
                  %msub
                    %mstyle{:scriptlevel => "0"}
                      %mo ′
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mu_1,\ldots, \mu_{p-1})\prec (\mu\prime_1,\ldots, \mu\prime_{p-1}),
          %p
            whilst if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi μ
                    %mi p
                  %mo =
                  %mi μ
                  %msub
                    %mstyle{:scriptlevel => "0"}
                      %mo ′
                    %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu_p = \mu\prime_p
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi μ
                    %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu_p
            is even,
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi μ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi μ
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≻
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi μ
                  %msub
                    %mstyle{:scriptlevel => "0"}
                      %mo ′
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %mi μ
                  %msub
                    %mstyle{:scriptlevel => "0"}
                      %mo ′
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mu_1,\ldots, \mu_{p-1})\succ (\mu\prime_1,\ldots, \mu\prime_{p-1}),
          %p
            where we adopt the notation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi μ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi μ
                    %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mu_1,\ldots, \mu_p)
            instead of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi μ
                      %mn 1
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mi …
                    %mo &lt;
                    %msub
                      %mi μ
                      %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mu_1\lt\ldots \lt \mu_p)
          %p For example, on our (2,2)-shuffles, the total order is
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 3
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 3
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 3
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (0,1) - (1,2) - (0,2) - (0,3) - (1,3) - (2,3).
          %p
            We note the lexicographic order on the sector with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi μ
                    %mn 2
                  %mo =
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu_2 = 2
            is reversed.
          %p For illustrative purposes, we will look at two other examples.
          %p
            First a generic
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi p
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (p,1)
            case: the shuffle poset for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi p
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (p,1)
            is, more or less,
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %mi p
                  %mo −
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %mi p
                  %mo −
                  %mn 4
                  %mo ,
                  %mi p
                  %mo −
                  %mn 3
                  %mo ,
                  %mi p
                  %mo −
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %mi p
                  %mo −
                  %mn 4
                  %mo ,
                  %mi p
                  %mo −
                  %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %mi p
                  %mo −
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo −
                  %mi …
                  %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %mi p
                  %mo −
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (0,\ldots , p-2) - (0,\ldots, p-4,p-3,p-1) - (0, \ldots, p-4,p-2,p-1) -
                  \\ldots  -  (1, \ldots , p-1)
          %p
            as, at each position, there is only one transposition that can be applied. The anti-lex total order will correspond
            %em exactly
            to this if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo −
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p-1
            is odd, but, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo −
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p-1
            is even, it will reverse the order on the last
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo −
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p-1
            positions giving
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %mi p
                  %mo −
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %mi p
                  %mo −
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %mi p
                  %mo −
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo −
                  %mi …
                  %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %mi p
                  %mo −
                  %mn 3
                  %mo ,
                  %mi p
                  %mo −
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (0,\ldots , p-2) - (1, \ldots , p-1) - (0,2, \ldots, p-1) -  \ldots  - (0,\ldots, p-3,p-1).
          %p
            There are various points to note. Firstly that if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            is even, the permutation corresponding to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %mi p
                  %mo −
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (1, \ldots , p-1)
            is odd. Secondly, the geometric picture is simply a prism,
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ×
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta[p]\times \Delta[1]
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ×
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ×
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\Delta[p]\times \Delta[1])\times \Delta[1]
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ×
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo ∪
                  %mo ∂
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ×
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\Delta[p]\times \Delta[1])\times \{0\} \cup \partial(\Delta[p]\times \Delta[1])\times \Delta[1],
          %p together with part of the top of the ‘canister’. (In general the total order seems to correspond to some sort of filling scheme although this is not always as clear as here.)
          %p
            Our second case will be (3,2). Again we will write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %mo ,
                  %mi j
                  %mo ,
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (i,j,k)
            instead of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                  %mo &lt;
                  %mi j
                  %mo &lt;
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i\lt j\lt k
            in order to save confusion over the various different orders being considered. The shuffle poset,
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Shuff
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 3
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Shuff}(3,2)
